/** Batch 61 — Tuesday 6 October 2026. Ten pieces from five lanes. Lead:
 *  "Two open models, and no weights yet" (Mistral Large 4 and Reflection's
 *  Beam), whose date is set to 17:55Z so the front zone, which leads with
 *  the newest article, puts it first (originally filed at 16:40Z).
 *  Source notes from each lane follow as written.
 *
 *  Part "ai" for Tuesday 6 October 2026. Two pieces: Mistral's and
 *  Reflection's open-weight launches, neither of which has shipped weights
 *  yet (AI), and the New York City Council's Committee of the Whole hearing
 *  on AI risk and the ten bills on its agenda (Policy).
 *
 *  AI / MISTRAL AND REFLECTION. Mistral, "Introducing Mistral Large 4"
 *  (mistral.ai/news/mistral-large-4, dated 6 October 2026), read in full:
 *  1 trillion parameters, 49 billion active; weights "end of this month";
 *  red-teaming with "cybersecurity leaders, vetted partners, and state
 *  authorities" who get "reduced moderation and expanded cyber
 *  capabilities"; 3,800 Grace Blackwell GPUs; 82% on the reproduce-and-patch
 *  test with Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra "near zero" because they refuse;
 *  highest refusal rate among open models on malicious cyber prompts; EUR 3bn
 *  Series D. TechCrunch (Anna Heim, 6 October) for Pierre Stock's quotes and
 *  his 4,000-GPU figure. The Decoder (6 October) for the Artificial Analysis
 *  Intelligence Index (ML4 38, Large 3 9, Claude Opus 5.5 Max 58; ML4 listed
 *  as proprietary until weights ship), the preview and list prices, and
 *  Mensch's remarks to the French parliamentary commission. TNW (6 October)
 *  for the 27 October weights date, Mistral's DeepSWE chart (ML4 62%, GLM-5.3
 *  61%, Beam 44%), and Lample's quote. Reflection, "Introducing Beam"
 *  (reflection.ai/blog/introducing-beam, dated 5 October 2026), read in
 *  full: 501B total, 23B active, 23.8T pretraining tokens, 10.5K GB300 GPUs
 *  for four weeks of RL, weights "later this month" under Apache 2.0, final
 *  red-teaming, waitlist, benchmark table (Beam DeepSWE 44.4, GLM 5.3 61.0,
 *  Kimi K3 68.0, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 74.2). TechCrunch (Rebecca Bellan, 5
 *  October) for the unverified status of the claims, ~$4.7bn raised per
 *  PitchBook, the $25bn pre-money valuation and the SpaceX/Nebius compute
 *  deals.
 *
 *  POLICY / NEW YORK CITY COUNCIL. NYC Council Legistar meeting detail for
 *  the Committee of the Whole, 5 October 2026, 11:00 AM (oversight item
 *  T2026-2573 and the bill summaries for T2026-2599 to T2026-2606, Int
 *  0161-2026 and Int 0504-2026; every bill marked "Laid Over by
 *  Committee"). TNW (5 October) for the testimony: Coxon, Kokotajlo,
 *  Turner; Graham, Dwyer, Friend, Cahill; Menin's catastrophic-risk and
 *  insurance questions; the council staff record of 13 incidents; SpaceXAI's
 *  absence and the subpoena. 6sqft (25 September) for the bills'
 *  announcement, the subpoena threats, Menin's statement and the Office of
 *  Algorithmic Accountability. PIX11 (via Yahoo, 6 October) for the fuller
 *  Coxon quote and the report that members expect to take the bills up on
 *  Thursday.
 *
 *  Heroes: Aleksandr Lyaptsev and Tomas Martinez via Unsplash, in
 *  part-ai.heroes.json; download endpoint triggered for both.
 *
 *  ----
 *
 *  Infra lane — Tuesday 6 October 2026. Two pieces: Micron's $600m
 *  patent settlement with Netlist (Markets) and Finland's order to halt
 *  work at two Google data centre sites (Technology).
 *
 *  MARKETS / MICRON-NETLIST. Netlist release "Netlist and Micron Enter
 *  Patent License and Settlement Agreements" (PR Newswire, 6 October 2026,
 *  08:00 ET) read in full. Netlist Form 8-K filed 6 October 2026 (Item
 *  1.01: agreements signed 5 October; worldwide, non-exclusive five-year
 *  licence to the entire portfolio; $30m a quarter for 20 quarters from Q4
 *  2026 to Q3 2031; Micron buys 10 million shares for $1m with a five-year
 *  staged lock-up). Netlist 8-K of 5 August 2026 for the Samsung deal
 *  ($239m upfront, up to $32.9m a quarter for 20 quarters, $300m-a-year
 *  supply agreement, ITC cooperation agreement, same 10m shares for $1m).
 *  Netlist 10-Q for the quarter to 27 June 2026 (legal proceedings: $445m
 *  verdict of 23 May 2024; PTAB decisions on the '912 claim 16 of 17 April
 *  2024 and the '417 of 30 July 2024; '912 appeal argued 9 September 2026;
 *  three Idaho bad-faith suits). Netlist Q2 2026 results release (30 July)
 *  for net sales and gross profit; 21 September 8-K (18 September AGM) for 333,893,680 shares
 *  outstanding at 22 July. Netlist releases of 24 and 29 September 2026 on
 *  the two ITC cases. Micron FQ4 2026 results release (30 September) for
 *  revenue. RTTNews (6 October) for the $5.65 NLST close and Micron
 *  pre-market price; Seeking Alpha (6 October, 9:37 ET) for the 18 per cent
 *  early rise. Reuters headline seen; article not readable.
 *  Own calculations labelled: share-price value of the 10m shares, Samsung
 *  gross maximum, ratios to Micron and Netlist figures.
 *
 *  TECHNOLOGY / GOOGLE FINLAND. Lupa- ja valvontavirasto (LVV) release of
 *  6 October 2026 read in Finnish (EIA Act s.32(2); halt immediately and by
 *  23 October; reply by 14 October; works covered and excluded; Muilu
 *  quotes; administrative coercion and conditional fine). Helsinki Times (6
 *  October) for the English rendering of Muilu's first quote, the EIA
 *  timetable, Google's compliance, the Ronander statement and the Finnish
 *  Association for Nature Conservation's 300-hectare figure. AFP via
 *  Moneycontrol (6 October) for the Ronander statement to AFP, Halmeenpaa,
 *  the four municipalities and Multala's Helsingin Sanomat remarks. CNBC
 *  (9 September 2026) for the €13bn investment, Porat, Fortum PPA, Orpo and
 *  the "Texas of Europe" quote. Muilu's second quote is Komposite's
 *  translation from the Finnish, labelled as such.
 *
 *  Heroes: Liam Briese and roya ann miller via Unsplash, in
 *  part-infra.heroes.json; download endpoint triggered for both.
 *
 *  ----
 *
 *  Part: crypto lane — Tuesday 6 October 2026. Two pieces: the CFTC's
 *  advance notice on crypto exchanges (Regulation CTX and Regulation CAM),
 *  in Crypto; and Rain's application for a national trust bank charter,
 *  filed one business day after community banks sued the OCC over those
 *  charters, in Fintech.
 *
 *  CRYPTO / CFTC CTX-CAM. CFTC release 9307-26 (5 October 2026) read in
 *  full. The ANPRM as approved by the Commission (cftc.gov/media/14716,
 *  RIN 3038-AF80, "subject to pre-publication technical corrections", 107
 *  pages plus Appendix A) read in full: s.III.A for "covered offer"
 *  (documentation incl. onboarding documents, terms and conditions, credit
 *  and margin documentation; offers may cover all transactions on an
 *  exchange or account; "acting in concert"), s.III.B-C for actual delivery
 *  (private keys; staking and governance; fully paid, open CTXs held only
 *  on an internal book-entry stay under the Act), s.IV.B(ii) for the
 *  question on electing not to offer CTXs, s.IV.C(ii) and fn.282 for
 *  on-chain protocols and liens ("may occur"), s.V.H(iv) for the same
 *  point worded "may not occur", s.V.A.1.d for eligible leverage providers,
 *  Appendix A for integrated CAM/FCM/DCO structures, s.I.B.3 for the 11
 *  comments in 2025 and the 4 December 2025 listing. Chairman Selig's WSJ
 *  op-ed as posted on cftc.gov (5 October). American Banker (Maria
 *  Volkova, 5 October) for the comment window and the point on permanence.
 *  Cointelegraph (6 October) for Better Markets' Benjamin Schiffrin quotes.
 *
 *  FINTECH / RAIN. Rain release on PR Newswire (5 October 2026) read in
 *  full, incl. its Q&A. Banking Dive (Gabrielle Saulsbery) via Yahoo Finance (6 October) for
 *  both applying on Monday, Modern Treasury not issuing a stablecoin,
 *  "first to be submitted" after the suit, and the complaint's "perversely
 *  allow" passage. Cointelegraph (6 October) for D.D.C. venue, Crypto
 *  Council for Innovation reaction. Gizmodo (6 October) for the complaint's
 *  count (21 trust banks, at least 13 crypto), Interpretive Letter 1176,
 *  the 2 March final rule, Protego, the receivership line, and Gould
 *  quotes. PYMNTS (2 October) for the ICBA and BPI statements, the OCC's
 *  final-rule language and its no-comment. Stablecoin supply: DefiLlama
 *  public stablecoins endpoint, USD-pegged circulating supply on 6 October
 *  2026 vs 6 October 2025 (percentage change is our calculation).
 *
 *  Heroes: Vladislav Maslow and Bogdan Radu via Unsplash, in
 *  part-crypto.heroes.json; download endpoint triggered for both.
 *
 *  ----
 *
 *  Markets lane — Tuesday 6 October 2026. Two pieces: DayOne Data Centers'
 *  Nasdaq IPO filing (Markets) and Blaize's collapsing revenue guidance
 *  (Business).
 *
 *  MARKETS / DAYONE. DayOne Data Centers Limited Form F-1, filed with the
 *  SEC on 5 October 2026 (CIK 2118192, accession 0001193125-26-414188),
 *  read: cover (ticker DODC, Nasdaq, underwriters, no size or price),
 *  prospectus summary (4.6GW Resources, 2.3GW Bookings, 962MW in service at
 *  20 September 2026, US$11.4bn cost to complete Bookings, delivery by end
 *  2028, "sufficient to fully fund" sentence, equity raised 2024-26,
 *  corporate history), summary financial data (revenue, net loss, SBC, cash,
 *  short-term investments, borrowings, capex payments), risk factors
 *  (largest customer 69.4% / 69.2%, second 12.3% / 15.1%, Malaysia 81.5% /
 *  87.0%, 30 October 2025 congressional letter, largest customer and
 *  geopolitical tensions, outbound investment rule and COINS Act, two
 *  customers on the CMC List, Entity List components, GDS 19.4% and
 *  director overlap, material weaknesses), liquidity section (US$1.82bn
 *  unutilised facilities), principal shareholders table. Straits Times
 *  (Bloomberg copy, 6 October, 08:43 SGT) for the confidential August filing
 *  and the up-to-US$5bn / ~US$20bn valuation report, the June Series C and
 *  the other data-centre IPO candidates. Business Times (6 October, 08:17
 *  SGT), read in full; cited for noting the CMC customers go unnamed.
 *  Calculations labelled as ours: two-customer share, capex/revenue ratio,
 *  cash plus short-term investments.
 *
 *  BUSINESS / BLAIZE. Blaize Holdings Form 8-K dated 5 October 2026 (event
 *  date 5 October; accession 0001871638-26-000063) and its Exhibit 99.1
 *  release, read in full. Blaize Q2 2026 release (8-K exhibit, 13 August
 *  2026) for H1 revenue, the earlier $40-43m guidance and the Munagala and
 *  Sehmi quotes. Form 10-Q for the quarter to 30 June 2026 (filed 13 August)
 *  for going concern, cash in Chinese banks, the equity facility, Starshine,
 *  BST and NeoTensr history, the receivables table and the May 2026
 *  offering at $1.85. 8-K of 24 September (restructuring, 26% of staff) and
 *  8-K of 28 September (Nasdaq bid-price notice, filed 2 October). Yahoo
 *  Finance (6 October, 14:33 UTC) for the Rosenblatt and Craig-Hallum
 *  downgrades. Prices from Nasdaq: Monday close $0.4359; $0.2882 at 1:45pm
 *  New York time on Tuesday. Calculations labelled as ours: implied Q4
 *  revenue, the move from $1.85, the market value estimate (144.8m shares
 *  outstanding at 13 August, 10-Q cover).
 *
 *  Heroes: Ven Jiun (Greg) Chee and Bermix Studio via Unsplash, in
 *  part-markets.heroes.json; download endpoint triggered for both.
 *
 *  ----
 *
 *  Enterprise lane — Tuesday 6 October 2026. Two pieces: Citrix's third
 *  exploited NetScaler zero-day in a week, in Cybersecurity, and the
 *  Texas attorney general's listing of the 2025 Oracle Health (Cerner)
 *  breach, in Business.
 *
 *  CYBERSECURITY / CITRIX NETSCALER. CISA KEV JSON feed, catalogue version
 *  2026.10.04, read 6 October: CVE-2026-88779 added 4 October, due 7
 *  October, forensicTriage "Yes", short description (denial of service),
 *  notes linking Citrix CTX697174 and the Citrix blog. NetScaler entries in
 *  the feed counted: six dated 2026 (30 March, 26 August, 9 September,
 *  27 September x2, 4 October). NVD API record for CVE-2026-88779
 *  (published 4 October 04:16 UTC): fixed builds, Citrix CVSS 4.0 score 8.7,
 *  NVD CVSS 3.1 score 7.5 with availability-only impact. Citrix's bulletin
 *  CTX697174 (changelog: initial publication 3 October PST) and blog read in
 *  a browser on 6 October; Citrix wording also carried by BleepingComputer
 *  (Lawrence Abrams, 4 October, updated 5 October) and SecurityWeek (Eduard
 *  Kovacs, 5 October). CISA BOD 26-04 implementation guidance read 6 October:
 *  forensic triage must be performed; its timeline is recommended, not required.
 *  BleepingComputer for the Friday reboot reports, the "upgrade again"
 *  instruction, the SAML preconditions and Beaumont's quote. SecurityWeek for
 *  the script description and watchTowr's reproduction and its view that the
 *  bug is used to speed exploitation of CVE-2026-88771. CyberScoop (Matt
 *  Kapko, 5 October) for Knott, Toomey and the Citrix spokesperson, and that
 *  Citrix declined to give customer numbers or first-exploitation date.
 *  SecurityWeek (earlier piece) for Mandiant's "since at least early
 *  September" and Palo Alto's ~50,000 exposed instances. Fixed builds for
 *  the earlier pair (14.1-73.37, 13.1-64.23) from our 28 September story.
 *  Weekdays checked by calendar.
 *
 *  BUSINESS / ORACLE HEALTH. Texas OAG Data Security Breach Reports listing,
 *  read 6 October via the live page with a search for "Cerner": Cerner
 *  Corporation, Kansas City, Missouri; Address, Social Security number,
 *  medical information; 2,992,244 Texans; notice provided, by U.S. Mail;
 *  published 10/02/2026. No "Oracle" entry. Texas Business & Commerce Code
 *  s.521.053(h)-(j) read on statutes.capitol.texas.gov. Bloomberg (5
 *  October) for the nearly 20 million figure, read through Gizmodo (6
 *  October) and MoneyCheck (6 October), which carry Bloomberg's reporting;
 *  MoneyCheck for Oracle declining further comment on Monday, Gizmodo for the
 *  DoD/VA client point (Gizmodo says only that it had asked Oracle).
 *  BleepingComputer (28 March 2025) for Oracle Health's customer notice,
 *  the 20 February / 22 January 2025 dates, the patient-notification
 *  position, the Verma signature and the extortion; BleepingComputer (21
 *  March 2025) for the separate Oracle Cloud SSO claim and Oracle's denial.
 *  Cerner purchase price ($28bn, 2022) via Yahoo Finance (6 October). Day
 *  count (589 days) and Texas share (about 15 per cent) are our
 *  calculations.
 *
 *  Heroes: Tyler and Maksym Kaharlytskyi via Unsplash, in
 *  part-enterprise.heroes.json; download endpoint triggered for both. */
const P = (dir, sectionLabel, sectionHref) => (o) => ({ dir, sectionLabel, sectionHref, ...o,
  photo: { file:"", origW:1, origH:1, alt:"", credit:"", capt:o.capt, creditLine:"Photograph via Unsplash" } });
const AI=P("ai","AI","ai"), PO=P("policy","Policy","policy"), MK=P("markets","Markets","markets"),
  TE=P("technology","Technology","technology"), CR=P("crypto","Crypto","crypto"), FT=P("fintech","Fintech","fintech"),
  BU=P("business","Business","business"), CY=P("cybersecurity","Cybersecurity","cybersecurity");

export default [

AI({ slug:"two-open-models-and-no-weights-yet", kick:"AI Models",
  headline:"Two open models, and no weights yet",
  dek:"Mistral and Reflection each launched an open-weight model this week pitched as the West's answer to China's. Neither has released the weights. Until Mistral does, its least restricted version is with governments and vetted partners.",
  metaDesc:"Mistral previewed Mistral Large 4 on 6 October 2026 and Reflection announced Beam on 5 October. Both are billed as open-weight models; both say the weights will follow later in October, after red-teaming.",
  author:"marcus-oyelaran", date:"2026-10-06T17:55:00Z",
  capt:"A hand adjusts circuit breakers in a lit electrical cabinet. The case for open weights is that the buyer runs the model on its own hardware, under its own rules.",
  tags:[{name:"Open Source",slug:"open-source"},{name:"AI Security",slug:"ai-security"},{name:"Enterprise AI",slug:"enterprise-ai"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/ai/a-switch-controlled-elsewhere/",kick:"AI Risk",title:"A switch controlled elsewhere",ago:"OCTOBER 4, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/the-export-control-that-is-not-in-the-licence/",kick:"AI Policy",title:"The export control that is not in the licence",ago:"SEPTEMBER 24, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/ten-days-after-the-pacing-call-the-price-fell/",kick:"AI Models",title:"Ten days after the pacing call, the price fell",ago:"SEPTEMBER 23, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `Two companies spent the start of this week claiming the same title. On Monday Reflection AI, a Brooklyn start-up founded in 2024 by two former Google DeepMind researchers, <a href="https://reflection.ai/blog/introducing-beam" rel="noopener">introduced Beam</a>, which it says "advances the Western open-weight frontier". On Tuesday Mistral, the Paris lab, <a href="https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-large-4/" rel="noopener">launched a public preview of Mistral Large 4</a>, which it says significantly outperforms "any open-weight model developed in the US or Europe". Both pitch themselves as the answer to the Chinese open models that have led the category. Neither model can yet be downloaded.`,
    `That is the detail the launch coverage tends to pass over. Mistral says it "will release the weights by the end of the month"; <a href="https://thenextweb.com/news/mistral-releases-large-4-a-1-trillion-parameter-open-weight-ai-model" rel="noopener">TNW</a> reported the date as 27 October. Until then the public can use the model only through Mistral's API. Reflection says Beam is "undergoing final red-teaming and evaluations", is open for now to "a select group of users" via a waitlist, and that the weights will follow "later this month" under an Apache 2.0 licence. Artificial Analysis, the independent benchmarking firm, still lists Mistral Large 4 as a proprietary model because its weights have not been released, <a href="https://the-decoder.com/mistral-large-4-is-said-to-be-the-most-powerful-open-ai-model-from-europe-and-the-u-s/" rel="noopener">The Decoder</a> reports.`,
    `The interval is not idle, and Mistral's description of it is the most revealing sentence in either announcement. Until the weights ship, it says, "we are red-teaming the model in real-world settings with cybersecurity leaders, vetted partners, and state authorities, who will access the same model with reduced moderation and expanded cyber capabilities." Pierre Stock, Mistral's vice-president of science, told <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/06/mistrals-new-1t-model-aims-to-leapfrog-closed-and-open-rivals/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a>: "In the meantime, we'll work with trusted partners and governments to make sure that the open source weights can be used to defend, but not to [perform] malicious attacks." For three weeks, in other words, the least restricted version of an open model is available only to a chosen few.`,
    `Cyber defence is the centre of Mistral's case, and by Mistral's own account its top score owes something to other companies' policies. On one test in the Artificial Analysis Cyber Index, which asks a model to reproduce a real vulnerability in open-source software and then patch it, Mistral says Large 4 scores 82 per cent, the highest of any model. "Several leading closed models, including Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra, score near zero on the same test because they refuse to perform the task," the company writes. The Decoder's verdict: "So the test measures provider policies as much as model capabilities." Mistral turns that into its pitch. "The cyber defense capabilities will enable enterprises and governments to defend themselves against threat actors that are jailbreaking closed models to perform cyber attacks," Guillaume Lample, its co-founder and chief scientist, said, according to TNW.`,
    `The same announcement also claims the opposite virtue. Mistral says Large 4's average refusal rate on malicious cyber prompts drawn from JailbreakBench, StrongREJECT and AgentHarm "is higher than all OSS models." It does not explain how the model separates a defender reproducing a flaw from an attacker doing the same thing; The Decoder noted the omission. The practical point is about who sets that line after release. Once the weights are public, TNW notes, "a bank or a government can download the model, run it on its own machines, and use it to protect its own networks without asking anyone's permission." Mistral's moderation applies to its own API. On a customer's hardware, the customer decides how the model is run.`,
    `That is also the sovereignty argument Mistral has been making for months. In May, its chief executive Arthur Mensch told a French parliamentary commission that the French military's codebases should not be scanned by Anthropic's Mythos, The Decoder reports. Mistral says Large 4 was trained from scratch on 3,800 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GPUs in its own European data centres, and that the preview runs on the same machines; Stock gave TechCrunch a figure of 4,000 GPUs, "which is two to three times less than our Chinese competitors, and significantly less than the closed source competitors." Christine Lagarde has made a related point, having <a href="/ai/a-switch-controlled-elsewhere/">cited the summer's suspension of two Anthropic models after a US export-control directive</a> as a warning for European finance.`,
    `On capability, independent measures put both launches some way short of the top. Artificial Analysis's Intelligence Index gives Large 4 38 points, up from 9 for Mistral Large 3, and ahead of Z.ai's GLM-5.2, but behind several Chinese open models and well behind Claude Opus 5.5 (Max) at 58, according to The Decoder. Reflection's own table is candid about its position: on DeepSWE, an agentic coding test, Beam scores 44.4 against 61.0 for GLM-5.3, 68.0 for Kimi K3 and 74.2 for DeepSeek V4.1 Flash. Mistral's chart, as reported by TNW, puts Large 4 at 62 per cent on the same test and Beam at 44. Reflection's argument is efficiency: it says Beam matches GLM-5.2 on advanced reasoning while using three to four times less inference compute. <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/05/reflection-debuts-beam-a-open-weight-ai-model-to-rival-chinese-models-at-lower-compute-cost/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a> noted that its performance claims have not been independently verified.`,
    `Both companies are well funded for a second attempt. Mistral calls Large 4 the first milestone on a roadmap funded by its €3bn Series D, which it calls the largest equity round raised by a European technology company, and that its reinforcement-learning run "is still in flight". Reflection has raised roughly $4.7bn, according to PitchBook data cited by TechCrunch, and has signed compute deals with SpaceX and Nebius worth more than $7bn. It says Beam's RL phase used 10,500 Nvidia GB300 GPUs for four weeks. Mistral is charging $0.68 per million input tokens and $2.09 per million output tokens during the preview, The Decoder reports, while its documentation lists double those rates.`,
    `The test for both comes later this month, and it is a simple one. An open-weight model is one whose weights anyone can download. Until 27 October for Mistral, the date TNW reported, and an unspecified date for Reflection, these are products with a promise attached, and the least restricted version of Mistral's is in the hands of partners and governments it has chosen.`,
  ] }),

PO({ slug:"new-york-city-wants-a-validator-for-every-model", kick:"AI Governance",
  headline:"New York's council wants a validator for every model",
  dek:"The city council's lead AI bill would make it unlawful to sell or deploy a model in New York without third-party validation and an off switch, at $25,000 per instance. On Monday it questioned the labs. None of their representatives raised a hand when asked who carried insurance against catastrophic risk.",
  metaDesc:"At a 5 October 2026 NYC Council Committee of the Whole hearing, Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and Meta testified as the council weighed ten AI bills, including Speaker Julie Menin's third-party validation and shut-down bill with a $25,000 per-instance penalty.",
  author:"dana-whitfield", date:"2026-10-06T17:20:00Z",
  capt:"New York City Hall, seen from City Hall Park. The full council sat as a Committee of the Whole on Monday for the first time since 2022.",
  tags:[{name:"Regulation",slug:"regulation"},{name:"AI Governance",slug:"ai-governance"},{name:"Policy",slug:"policy"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/ai/anthropic-invites-the-examiners-in/",kick:"Governance",title:"Anthropic invites the examiners inside",ago:"SEPTEMBER 13, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/a-finra-for-the-frontier/",kick:"AI Governance",title:"A FINRA for the frontier",ago:"SEPTEMBER 26, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/sarbanes-oxley-without-the-filing/",kick:"AI Governance",title:"Sarbanes-Oxley without the filing",ago:"OCTOBER 1, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `The most consequential document from New York City Hall on Monday was not the testimony. It was a one-paragraph bill summary. Under a bill from the council speaker, Julie Menin, it would be "unlawful to market, offer for sale, sell, or deploy an artificial intelligence (AI) model in New York City that has not received third-party validation or does not have a technical capability to be shut down by a human operator," according to the <a href="https://legistar.council.nyc.gov/MeetingDetail.aspx?ID=1445976&amp;GUID=325D66DF-B99B-4C9A-8061-6EC9907142F2&amp;Options=info%7C&amp;Search=" rel="noopener">council's own record</a> of the hearing. The penalty is a fixed $25,000 "per instance."`,
    `The bill was one of ten on the agenda of a Committee of the Whole, the full 51-member council sitting as one committee, which it had not done since 2022, <a href="https://thenextweb.com/news/nyc-council-ai-hearing-coxon-kokotajlo-turner" rel="noopener">TNW</a> reports. Anthropic sent Logan Graham, head of its Frontier Red Team; OpenAI sent Morgan Dwyer, its head of policy development and operations; Alice Friend of Google and Shane Cahill of Meta appeared by video. Google, OpenAI and Anthropic agreed to attend only after the council warned them of subpoenas, TNW says. SpaceXAI, which the council did subpoena, sent no one, and Menin said she would ask a judge to enforce it.`,
    `Validation under Menin's bill would cover task performance, disparate impact, data privacy and safety, including whether the shut-down capability works. Validators would have to certify to the developer and to the city's Cyber Command whether a model is ready for deployment, and disclose any interest they hold in it. Cyber Command would write the rules, including who qualifies as a validator. A companion bill from Council Member Carl Wilson would require any advertisement for an AI model in the city to disclose whether it has been validated, and would bar materially false or misleading claims about a model's safety, with civil penalties of up to $25,000.`,
    `The idea is not foreign to the companies in the room. Anthropic's chief executive committed last month to give outside evaluators <a href="/ai/anthropic-invites-the-examiners-in/">employee-level access</a>, and Sam Altman said OpenAI would do the same. The accord six AI chiefs signed with the President last week asks each company for <a href="/ai/sarbanes-oxley-without-the-filing/">an outside auditor</a>. Those are undertakings the companies chose. New York's version would make validation a condition of selling in the city, with qualifications set by a city agency. Neither the bill summary nor the bill text defines what counts as an "instance" for a model offered to millions of users; on our reading, that definition will decide whether $25,000 is a nuisance or a deterrent.`,
    `Menin pressed the four company witnesses on two questions, TNW reports. First, she asked each to put a number on the risk of a catastrophic worst case. OpenAI's Dwyer said the exact figure did not matter because no level of that risk was acceptable. "We should not train models that we cannot make an extremely strong case that we can keep under human control," Dwyer said. Menin called the answer "flippant at best". Friend said forecasting catastrophic risk "is not a perfect science at this stage". Second, Menin asked who carried insurance against catastrophic risks. None of the four raised a hand. Graham said Anthropic welcomes "smart regulation" and that state and local governments have a role; Friend backed a "comprehensive" federal framework.`,
    `The other witnesses were former insiders. "On the current path, I think it is more likely than not that humanity loses control to these AIs, and it could end in human extinction," said Jacob Coxon, a former OpenAI and Anthropic researcher who left Anthropic in September, according to TNW. <a href="https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/nyc-advances-package-bills-regulate-104244800.html" rel="noopener">PIX11</a> reported that he also told the council AI could bring "tremendous upsides" if it goes well. Daniel Kokotajlo and Alex Turner, formerly of OpenAI and Google DeepMind, testified remotely under subpoena. Council staff published a table of 13 publicly reported AI incidents in 2026 involving models from the four companies, and exhibits setting each company's public safety claims beside later incidents, TNW says.`,
    `The rest of the package targets the gaps those incidents exposed. A bill from Council Member Kamillah Hanks would require city contractors and agencies to report AI safety incidents to Cyber Command within 24 hours, and Cyber Command to disclose them publicly within 24 hours, a contrast with the <a href="/ai/eighty-four-days-before-anyone-was-told/">84 days</a> it took for an OpenAI model's entry into an Australian government portal to be reported. Virginia Maloney's bill would let people sue model providers for foreseeable harm from third-party misuse where the company failed to put reasonable safeguards in place. Menin's second bill would pay anyone who reports an AI violation 25 per cent of what the city recovers, or 50 per cent if they bring the case themselves. Kevin Riley's would extend the city's whistleblower law to AI; others cover chatbot privacy, AI's effect on city jobs, AI emergency planning and deepfakes of officials and candidates.`,
    `None of it has passed. Every bill on the agenda was laid over by the committee, the council's record shows, and PIX11 reported that members expect to take them up on Thursday. Menin has framed the effort as filling a federal gap; when the bills were announced in September she cited Washington's "failure" to enact meaningful rules, <a href="https://www.6sqft.com/nyc-council-announces-slate-of-bills-aimed-at-regulating-ai/" rel="noopener">6sqft</a> reported, while saying: "NYC is fast becoming the technology and AI capital of the world, which we want to encourage." Coxon told the council measures like these "may be helpful in the short term", TNW reported. The labs have said they want outside checks. New York's council is proposing to put a price on deploying without them.`,
  ] }),

MK({ slug:"thirty-million-dollars-a-quarter", kick:"Semiconductors",
  headline:"Thirty million dollars a quarter",
  dek:"Micron will pay Netlist $600m over five years to end every case between them, six days after Netlist asked the ITC to block Micron's high-bandwidth memory and named Nvidia, Broadcom and Google as respondents.",
  metaDesc:"Micron and Netlist signed a settlement and five-year patent licence on 5 October 2026: $30m a quarter, $600m in all, plus 10 million Netlist shares for $1m. The deal settles all pending legal proceedings between the two.",
  author:"jonathan-bright", date:"2026-10-06T16:30:00Z",
  capt:"A server memory module. Netlist's patents cover the modules that carry DRAM chips in servers and the high-bandwidth memory stacked beside AI accelerators; Micron now has a five-year licence to all of them.",
  tags:[{name:"Semiconductors",slug:"semiconductors"},{name:"AI Infrastructure",slug:"ai-infrastructure"},{name:"Capital Markets",slug:"capital-markets"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/markets/the-deposits-arrive-before-the-bits/",kick:"Semiconductors",title:"The deposits arrive before the bits",ago:"SEPTEMBER 24, 2026"},
    {href:"/markets/a-round-number-in-won/",kick:"Semiconductors",title:"Eight hundred and sixty billion is a round number in won",ago:"SEPTEMBER 27, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/samsung-openai-chip-partnership-deepens/",kick:"Semiconductors",title:"Samsung moves up OpenAI's chip stack",ago:"SEPTEMBER 10, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `Micron Technology has settled its patent war with Netlist, the small Californian memory company that has been suing it since 2021. Under agreements signed on Monday and <a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/netlist-and-micron-enter-patent-license-and-settlement-agreements-302899647.html" rel="noopener">announced by Netlist</a> on Tuesday, the two sides drop all pending proceedings against each other and Micron takes a five-year licence to Netlist's patent portfolio, including patents on server memory modules and high-bandwidth memory. Micron will pay $30m a quarter from the fourth quarter of 2026 to the third quarter of 2031, $600m in all.`,
    `The timing is the story. On 29 September Netlist announced that it had <a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/netlist-files-new-itc-action-against-micron-technology-inc-involving-hbm-patents-for-ai-computing-302892194.html" rel="noopener">filed a complaint</a> at the US International Trade Commission seeking exclusion and cease-and-desist orders against Micron and three of its customers: Nvidia, Broadcom and Google. Netlist alleged that two of its patents cover at least Micron's HBM3E, HBM4 and HBM4E products, the memory stacked beside AI accelerators. Six days later Micron signed. Six days before that, on 23 September, the ITC had <a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/us-international-trade-commission-votes-to-institute-investigation-into-micron-technology-inc-302888468.html" rel="noopener">instituted a separate investigation</a> into whether Micron's DDR5 server modules should be banned from import, with Supermicro, Hewlett Packard Enterprise and Lenovo named as respondents alongside Micron. ITC cases commonly reach trial within a year, Netlist noted at the time. Netlist's release does not say why Micron settled when it did. It says only that the two sides will settle and mutually release all pending legal proceedings between them. In both ITC complaints Netlist had sought orders against Micron's customers as well as Micron.`,
    `The money is small on one side of the table and large on the other. Micron reported <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/723125/000072312526000018/a2026q4ex991-pressrelease.htm" rel="noopener">revenue of $54.23bn</a> for its fiscal fourth quarter, which ended on 3 September. On Komposite's arithmetic, a $30m quarterly payment is about 0.06 per cent of that. Netlist reported <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1282631/000110465926088464/tm2621423d1_ex99-1.htm" rel="noopener">net sales of $109.8m</a> and gross profit of $22.9m for its second quarter, to 27 June. One Micron instalment is larger than that quarter's entire gross profit.`,
    `Netlist's <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1282631/000110465926113897/tm2626949d1_8k.htm" rel="noopener">Form 8-K</a> adds two details the release left out. The licence covers Netlist's entire worldwide patent portfolio, not just the memory-module and HBM families, and it is non-exclusive and cannot be sublicensed. Micron has also bought 10 million Netlist shares for $1m in cash, or ten cents a share, released from a lock-up in fifths on each anniversary over five years. Netlist's shares closed at $5.65 on Monday, according to RTTNews. On that price, Komposite calculates, the stake was worth about $56.5m. Netlist had 333.9 million shares outstanding in July.`,
    `That clause is not new. In August Netlist settled with Samsung on a <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1282631/000110465926090641/tm2622153d1_8k.htm" rel="noopener">similar template</a>: a five-year licence, a settlement of pending cases, and 10 million shares sold to a Samsung affiliate for $1m with the same five-year lock-up. Samsung's deal was richer. It agreed to pay $239m upfront and quarterly fees of up to $32.9m for twenty quarters, set by a revenue-based formula, before Korean withholding tax. On Komposite's arithmetic that is up to about $897m gross. Samsung also gave Netlist the right to buy up to $300m of DRAM and NAND a year from it, and signed an "ITC Cooperation Agreement" under which it agreed to provide information, documents or declarations for Netlist's future ITC actions against third parties. Micron's deal has no upfront payment and, in the 8-K, no supply or cooperation agreement. Its fee is fixed rather than tied to revenue.`,
    `Netlist's chief executive, C.K. Hong, had already said what the Samsung deal was for. "The Samsung agreement established an important industry benchmark for the value of our IP," he said when the DDR5 investigation opened. "We intend to continue our enforcement efforts against unauthorized users such as Micron." On Tuesday he said: "The agreement with Micron further validates the value of our AI memory technologies."`,
    `What Netlist gave up was a jury award that was far from certain. Netlist's <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1282631/000110465926094127/nlst-20260627x10q.htm" rel="noopener">latest quarterly report</a> records that on 23 May 2024 a federal jury in the Eastern District of Texas found that Micron had wilfully infringed two Netlist patents and awarded $445m. The court denied Micron's post-trial motions in June 2025 and Micron appealed. But the Patent Trial and Appeal Board had by then found claim 16 of one of the two patents unpatentable and all challenged claims of the other unpatentable. Netlist was appealing both decisions, and the appeals were scheduled for oral argument on 9 September. The company told investors that collecting the award could depend on those appeals. Micron, for its part, had filed three suits in Idaho state court accusing Netlist of asserting patents in bad faith. The 8-K says the settlement releases "all pending legal proceedings between the Company and Micron identified therein".`,
    `The market took the settlement as good news for the smaller company. Netlist shares jumped 18 per cent in early trading, <a href="https://seekingalpha.com/news/4650546-netlist-surges-as-micron-agrees-to-pay-600m-for-five-year-license-agreement" rel="noopener">Seeking Alpha</a> reported. Micron was trading at $1,069.60 before the bell, up 0.53 per cent, according to <a href="https://www.rttnews.com/3696851/netlist-enters-into-patent-license-and-settlement-agreements-with-micron-technology.aspx" rel="noopener">RTTNews</a>.`,
    `Netlist's patent fights are not over. Its own release still lists litigation with Google as a risk, and Google, Nvidia and Broadcom were named in the HBM complaint as Micron's customers. Samsung and Micron, two of the three big DRAM makers, now hold Netlist licences that run into 2031, and both pay in quarterly instalments. Micron's alone come to $120m a year. Netlist earned $1.4m of net income in its second quarter. For Micron, each quarterly payment is less than a tenth of one per cent of a quarter's sales, and the full $600m is about 1.1 per cent of one quarter's revenue, by Komposite's arithmetic.`,
  ] }),

TE({ slug:"the-forest-came-down-before-the-assessment", kick:"Data Centers",
  headline:"The forest came down before the assessment",
  dek:"Finland's supervisory agency has told Google's local company to stop clearing, blasting and digging at two data centre sites until environmental assessments are finished. Google says it will comply.",
  metaDesc:"Finland's LVV told Google's Tuike Finland on 6 October 2026 to halt work that significantly alters the environment at its Muhos and Kajaani data centre sites by 23 October, until environmental impact assessments are completed.",
  author:"elena-vasquez", date:"2026-10-06T17:15:00Z",
  capt:"A stump in a clear-cut forest. The Finnish Association for Nature Conservation says more than 300 hectares were cleared at Google's planned site in Muhos while the environmental assessment was still under way.",
  tags:[{name:"Data Centers",slug:"data-centers"},{name:"Regulation",slug:"regulation"},{name:"Energy for Computing",slug:"energy-for-computing"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/business/data-center-boom-local-economies/",kick:"Data Centers",title:"The data center boom collides with local economics",ago:"AUGUST 4, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/five-years-for-a-wire-six-for-a-turbine/",kick:"Power",title:"Five years for a wire, six for a turbine",ago:"SEPTEMBER 24, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/the-trade-the-grid-needs-did-not-grow/",kick:"Data Centers",title:"The trade the grid needs did not grow",ago:"SEPTEMBER 27, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `Four weeks after Google called Finland the site of its largest single investment in Europe, a Finnish regulator has told it to stop digging. On Tuesday the Finnish Supervisory Agency, known by its Finnish initials LVV, <a href="https://lvv.fi/-/lupa-ja-valvontavirasto-kehottaa-tuike-finland-oy-ta-keskeyttamaan-muhoksen-ja-kajaanin-datakeskushankkeiden-valmistelutoimet" rel="noopener">called on Tuike Finland</a>, the Google company developing data centres at Muhos and Kajaani, to suspend preparatory work that significantly alters the environment at both sites. It must stop immediately and no later than 23 October, and keep the work suspended until the projects' environmental impact assessments are complete.`,
    `The request is precise about what has to stop. It covers, among other things, tree removal, the stripping of topsoil, excavation, blasting, crushing, moving soil, drainage and building site roads and storage areas. Planning, surveying, soil investigations and other work with minor, easily reversible effects can continue. The agency relied on section 32(2) of Finland's Environmental Impact Assessment Act. Tuike Finland has until 14 October to give a written explanation and to say whether it intends to comply. If it does not, the agency says it will consider administrative coercion under the EIA Act or other applicable law, which can be backed by a conditional fine.`,
    `Google will not need to be forced. The company has said it will comply, <a href="https://www.helsinkitimes.fi/business/29355-google-ordered-to-halt-data-centre-works-in-finland-over-environmental-concerns.html" rel="noopener">Helsinki Times</a> reported. "We understand the concerns and have fallen short of our own high standards in this instance," Sondre Ronander, a Google spokesman, said in a statement to AFP, <a href="https://www.moneycontrol.com/world/finland-halts-work-at-two-google-data-centres-over-missing-environmental-impact-assessments-article-14046045.html" rel="noopener">reported by Moneycontrol</a>. He said the company had "acted in good faith" and carried out environmental surveys and measures. "As we continue our long-term work in the region, we are advancing our landscaping and biodiversity plans, which include tree planting across 130 hectares at Muhos," he said.`,
    `The problem is the order of events. The agency says reports available to it show that large-scale preparatory work has already been done at both sites: trees removed, topsoil stripped, site roads and storage areas built, ditches and culverts altered and stormwater arrangements made. "Our assessment is that these measures change the environment and cause impacts whose identification and assessment are among the central objectives of the environmental impact assessment procedure," said Tommi Muilu, head of the agency's environment department, in Helsinki Times's translation. Neither assessment is finished; both are expected to conclude during 2026. Muilu's second point goes to the purpose of the law. "Large-scale preparatory measures that change the environment can weaken the advance steering effect of the EIA procedure and narrow the opportunities to examine alternatives and prevent harmful environmental effects," he said, in Komposite's translation from the Finnish.`,
    `In other words, an assessment that is meant to shape where and how a project is built loses its point once the ground has been cleared. Google's defence rests on a different statute. Ronander has said Google acted in good faith under Finland's Forest Act, conducted nature surveys and took measures to protect sites with environmental value. The regulator is applying the environmental assessment law.`,
    `The complaint came from outside. The Finnish Association for Nature Conservation said in September that more than 300 hectares of forest had been cleared at the Muhos site, at Leppiniemi, while the assessment was still incomplete, and it raised concerns about Google's Otanmäki project in Kajaani. Its chair, Hanna Halmeenpaa, told AFP that "nature sites which should be preserved" had been logged. The 130 hectares Google plans to plant with trees at Muhos is less than half the area the association says was cleared there, on Komposite's comparison of the two figures.`,
    `The political cost may be time. Sari Multala, Finland's minister of the environment and climate, said Google's projects would probably be delayed by the problems with the assessments, AFP reported. In an interview with Helsingin Sanomat on Sunday, she called it "a serious matter" if the company and its subcontractors had acted as claimed.`,
    `Finland had sold itself on speed and supply. When Google <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/09/google-finland-ai-infrastructure-investment.html" rel="noopener">announced on 9 September</a> that it would invest at least €13bn in Finnish AI infrastructure through 2028, CNBC described the country's appeal as land and power that are in short supply in most of Europe. The plan covers data centres in Muhos, Vaala, Kajaani and Hamina, where Google already runs one, and a 22-year life-extension power purchase agreement with Fortum, the Finnish utility. "Finland is seeing huge demand for AI infrastructure right now, I've heard it called the 'Texas of Europe' at industry events," Matti Lajunen, a real-estate partner at the law firm Hannes Snellman, told CNBC. Prime minister Petteri Orpo welcomed the deal.`,
    `Ruth Porat, Alphabet's president and chief investment officer, made a promise that the regulator has now tested. "This investment underscores Google's commitment to grow our presence responsibly, pairing the expansion of our technical infrastructure with new energy capacity, grid enhancements, and energy affordability initiatives," she said. Building data centres is a race for power connections and permits, and developers can save time by preparing the ground before every approval is in. Finland's regulator has now told one developer to stop clearing ground until its environmental assessment is finished. For the other developers now looking at Finland, the request suggests that this step cannot be skipped.`,
  ] }),

CR({ slug:"the-offer-of-leverage-is-the-switch", kick:"Regulation",
  headline:"The offer of leverage is the switch",
  dek:"The CFTC cannot make crypto exchanges register with it. Its first crypto plan works through one word instead: on its preliminary reading, an exchange that offers customers leverage would bring even their fully paid trades under federal rules, until the coins leave for a wallet the customer controls.",
  metaDesc:"The CFTC's 5 October 2026 advance notice on Regulation CTX and Regulation CAM reads 'offer' broadly: offering leverage would put even fully paid retail crypto trades under its rules until actual delivery. Comments run 60 days.",
  author:"marcus-oyelaran", date:"2026-10-06T16:40:00Z",
  capt:"A hand points at currency quotes on a phone in front of a laptop. The CFTC's plan would cover retail trades on exchanges that offer leverage, including trades customers pay for in full.",
  tags:[{name:"Regulation",slug:"regulation"},{name:"Digital Assets",slug:"digital-assets"},{name:"Market Structure",slug:"market-structure"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/crypto/the-clarity-act-stops-at-forty-nine/",kick:"Regulation",title:"The CLARITY Act stops at forty-nine",ago:"SEPTEMBER 16, 2026"},
    {href:"/crypto/nine-of-ten-new-clearing-houses-never-lend/",kick:"Market Structure",title:"Nine of the ten new clearing houses never lend",ago:"SEPTEMBER 30, 2026"},
    {href:"/markets/the-margin-is-named-in-the-rule/",kick:"Market Structure",title:"The margin is named in the rule",ago:"SEPTEMBER 29, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `On Monday the Commodity Futures Trading Commission published what its chairman, Michael Selig, called its "first round of regulations for crypto markets". It is not yet a rule. The <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/PressRoom/PressReleases/9307-26" rel="noopener">release</a> describes an advance notice of proposed rulemaking, and says the comments it draws will inform "potential future agency action, such as a rulemaking". The notice sketches two pieces: Regulation Crypto Asset Transactions, or CTX, and Regulation Crypto Asset Markets, or CAM. Comments are due 60 days after it appears in the Federal Register.`,
    `"Unlike the Clarity Act, these regulations wouldn't require crypto assets to trade on CFTC-registered platforms," he wrote in a <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/PressRoom/SpeechesTestimony/seligstatement100526" rel="noopener">Wall Street Journal op-ed</a> the agency posted on Monday. "We don't have the authority to impose such a requirement without congressional action." The plan instead offers what he calls a purpose-fit option for exchanges that want one federal regulator. Its selling point is leverage: "Unlike state-licensed exchanges, these exchanges would be permitted to allow retail customers to trade on a margined, leveraged or financed basis."`,
    `The authority rests on a provision added by the Dodd-Frank Act in 2010, section 2(c)(2)(D) of the Commodity Exchange Act. It says a retail commodity transaction that is entered into, or "offered (even if not entered into)", on a leveraged, margined or financed basis must be traded on a regulated exchange as if it were a <a href="/markets/the-basis-trade-meets-a-haircut/">futures contract</a>. The main way out is "actual delivery" of the commodity within 28 days. Section III of the <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/media/14716/ANPRM_CTX-CAM_asapproved/download" rel="noopener">107-page notice</a> sets out how the Commission would read those words for crypto, and the reading is wide.`,
    `Start with the offer. The Commission says it preliminarily believes an offer of leverage "attaches to all agreements, contracts, or transactions to which it is applicable", whether or not any one trade uses borrowed money. Such an offer can be made in "standard customer documentation such as customer onboarding documents", exchange terms and conditions, or credit and margin documents. It can cover "all transactions available on an exchange" or "all transactions available through a customer account". Financing also counts when it comes from someone "acting in concert" with the exchange. That can include a lender the exchange advertises, shares revenue with, or offers through its own financing platform.`,
    `Then delivery. A customer who turns leverage down and pays in full still holds what the notice calls a "fully paid, open CTX". It stays under the Commission's futures-style rules for as long as it is "only recorded on an internal book-entry of the exchange, prior to actual delivery". Delivery, in the Commission's preliminary view, may require possession of the private keys to the wallet that holds the asset. For governance tokens, it may also mean voting directly; for staking, staking "without being subject to fees charged by an intermediary". On this reading, a coin left in an account at an exchange that offers margin would be a regulated position. Once it moves to a wallet the customer controls, the Commission keeps only its anti-fraud and anti-manipulation powers.`,
    `That makes the offer a switch the exchange controls. The notice asks commenters directly whether its reading would leave "a commercially viable avenue for an exchange to elect to not offer CTXs and thereby continue to operate under the state-level regulatory framework". An exchange that wants to offer retail customers leverage takes the whole package. One that does not can stay with state money-transmitter licences, which the notice spends several pages arguing protect retail customers poorly.`,
    `A CAM would be a new kind of designated contract market that lists only crypto transactions. Every trade would have to go through a futures commission merchant and be cleared by a derivatives clearing organisation. Leverage could come only from a futures commission merchant or from a bank that one sponsors. The notice would also let a single firm hold all three registrations, exchange, broker and clearing house, which it calls a "Fully Integrated CAM". The notice says crypto firms typically run integrated businesses of this kind. Better Markets says affiliations like these contributed to FTX's collapse.`,
    `For decentralised venues, the Commission says it preliminarily understands that trades on on-chain protocols "typically result in a purchaser obtaining possession and control" of the asset, which would satisfy actual delivery. In one passage it adds that case law treats a lien and control as separate questions, "such that actual delivery may occur where a lien or security interest remains outstanding". A footnote gives the example of a protocol that liquidates a margined position when its collateral falls too far. Near the end of the document, the same point appears with the opposite wording: actual delivery "may not occur where a lien or security interest remains outstanding". The text is marked as subject to pre-publication technical corrections. Which wording the Commission means matters for whether margined on-chain trades could count as delivered.`,
    `The groundwork is thin. When the Commission asked last year how exchanges could list these transactions, it received 11 comments, the notice says. The first such trades on a US regulated exchange were announced on 4 December 2025, with no rulebook written for them. Better Markets, which argues retail investors would be safer under the Securities and Exchange Commission, has already objected. "Unlike the SEC, the CFTC lacks an investor protection mandate," Benjamin Schiffrin, its director of securities policy, said, as quoted by <a href="https://cointelegraph.com/news/better-markets-criticize-cftc-regulate-retail-crypto" rel="noopener">Cointelegraph</a>. "Because the CFTC's rules lack the protections that apply when investors trade securities regulated by the SEC, the CFTC is the wrong agency to regulate transactions involving crypto assets by retail customers."`,
    `Selig has his own caveat. "We haven't solved every problem, nor can agency action substitute indefinitely for a statutory framework passed by Congress, but we must do what we can," he wrote. Industry insiders say, according to <a href="https://www.americanbanker.com/news/cftc-seeks-input-on-leveraged-crypto-trading-rules" rel="noopener">American Banker</a>, that rules adopted by the SEC and CFTC may not have the permanence of legislation and could be more easily changed by future administrations. Even the reach of these rules depends on a choice exchanges make for themselves: whether to offer customers leverage.`,
  ] }),

FT({ slug:"rain-applies-for-the-charter-the-community-banks-are-suing-over", kick:"Stablecoins",
  headline:"Rain applies for the charter the community banks are suing over",
  dek:"The stablecoin card company wants a national trust bank to hold reserves and issue coins. It filed one business day after community bankers sued the OCC over that kind of charter, and its pitch leans on the word the lawsuit is about.",
  metaDesc:"Rain applied on 5 October 2026 for an OCC national trust charter to custody assets, manage stablecoin reserves and issue stablecoins, one business day after the ICBA sued the OCC over trust charters for crypto firms.",
  author:"dana-whitfield", date:"2026-10-06T17:20:00Z",
  capt:"The handle of an old strongbox. Rain says its proposed trust bank would hold client assets as a fiduciary and would not lend or reuse the reserves behind any stablecoin it issues.",
  tags:[{name:"Stablecoins",slug:"stablecoins"},{name:"Banking",slug:"banking"},{name:"Regulation",slug:"regulation"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/crypto/the-clarity-act-stops-at-forty-nine/",kick:"Regulation",title:"The CLARITY Act stops at forty-nine",ago:"SEPTEMBER 16, 2026"},
    {href:"/crypto/ninety-one-per-cent-on-the-chain-stripe-built/",kick:"Stablecoins",title:"Ninety-one per cent on the chain Stripe built",ago:"OCTOBER 1, 2026"},
    {href:"/crypto/the-adviser-holds-the-keys-when-no-one-else-will/",kick:"Custody",title:"The adviser holds the keys when no one else will",ago:"OCTOBER 3, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `Rain, a New York company whose partners run stablecoin card, wallet and payments programmes, said on Monday that it had applied to the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency to set up Rain National Trust Bank. According to its <a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/rain-files-application-with-the-occ-to-establish-rain-national-trust-bank-302898950.html" rel="noopener">announcement</a>, the bank would have three lines of business: custody of digital assets and dollars, management of reserves for stablecoin issuers permitted under the GENIUS Act, and issuing and redeeming dollar stablecoins as issuer of record. Modern Treasury, a San Francisco payments company, applied for a <a href="/fintech/stablecoin-trust-bank-charters/">trust charter</a> the same day, <a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/crypto/articles/rain-modern-treasury-seek-occ-153437247.html" rel="noopener">Banking Dive</a> reported. Unlike Rain, it does not plan to issue a stablecoin of its own.`,
    `Banking Dive says these were the first trust charter applications sent to the OCC since the Independent Community Bankers of America sued the agency on Friday 2 October. The trade group filed in federal court in Washington against the OCC and the Comptroller, Jonathan Gould. It asks the court to throw out the agency's 2 March chartering rule and Interpretive Letter 1176, the 2021 letter that rule builds on, and to stop the OCC using either to approve more charters. By the complaint's count, as reported by <a href="https://gizmodo.com/small-banks-sue-to-block-bank-charters-for-coinbase-ripple-and-other-crypto-companies-2000821762" rel="noopener">Gizmodo</a>, the OCC has approved or conditionally approved 21 trust banks under the Trump administration, at least 13 of them crypto companies.`,
    `The case turns on one distinction. "The OCC's decision to allow companies to obtain national trust bank charters to conduct substantial non-fiduciary activities exceeds the authority Congress granted the agency," the ICBA's chief executive, Rebeca Romero Rainey, said in the statement announcing the suit, quoted by <a href="https://www.pymnts.com/legal/bank-regulation/2026/icba-sues-occ-to-stop-alleged-fast-track-of-crypto-bank-charters/" rel="noopener">PYMNTS</a>. "Congress did not create the national trust charter as a side door into the banking system for crypto firms seeking the credibility of a federal bank charter without the Community Reinvestment Act obligations, consolidated supervision, capital and liquidity standards, and FDIC insurance that apply to insured depository institutions."`,
    `The OCC's position is that nothing new has happened. When it issued the March rule, it said the rule clarified "the longstanding authority of national banks limited to the operations of trust companies and activities related thereto to engage in non-fiduciary activities in addition to their fiduciary activities." It added: "The final rule would neither expand nor contract the OCC's authority to charter a national bank." A spokesperson told PYMNTS the agency does not comment on litigation.`,
    `Read against that dispute, Rain's announcement is built around the word "fiduciary". "The institutions building on Rain want the assets behind their programs held by a fiduciary that answers to a federal regulator," said Farooq Malik, its chief executive and co-founder. The proposed bank, he said, "will be a separate subsidiary that holds client assets in custody, as a fiduciary and subject to OCC examination." The release lists what the bank would not do. It would be uninsured, take no deposits, make no commercial loans and offer no consumer accounts. Client assets would be "custody assets held for identified owners, not liabilities of the bank", and the reserves behind any stablecoin it issued "will not be pledged, lent, or reused". The release calls the custody business fiduciary. It does not use the word for the other two lines, managing other issuers' reserves and issuing and redeeming stablecoins.`,
    `Rain has also chosen a chief executive who has been through the chartering process before. Brandon Soto, the proposed head, was chief financial officer of Square Financial Services, Block's Utah industrial bank, where he helped prepare the charter application the FDIC and Utah's regulator approved. "A trust bank's first job is simple. Know what you hold, know who you hold it for, and keep it safe," he said. "I have sat across from bank examiners for twenty years, and the best answer you can give is, 'Here's the reconciliation.'"`,
    `The banks' other complaint is about risk if one of these firms fails. The complaint says, according to Gizmodo, that a national trust charter would let Protego Holdings, the crypto firm whose conditional approval it asks the court to vacate, operate nationwide and preempt a range of state consumer-protection laws without FDIC insurance, Community Reinvestment Act duties or Basel-style capital and liquidity rules. It also says the OCC has not run a receivership of an uninsured bank in nearly 100 years. The Bank Policy Institute, which represents large banks, took a similar line in a statement on Friday, quoted by PYMNTS. "Companies should not receive trust charters unless they plan to limit their operations to genuine trust activities," said Paige Pidano Paridon, a co-head of regulatory affairs. "If they want to engage in traditional banking activities, they should seek full-service banking charters." The Crypto Council for Innovation called the suit an attempt to stifle innovation, <a href="https://cointelegraph.com/news/rain-seeks-us-trust-bank-charter-days-after-occ-sued-over-crypto-charters" rel="noopener">Cointelegraph</a> reported.`,
    `The stakes are large. Dollar stablecoins in circulation came to about $312.7bn on Tuesday, according to <a href="https://defillama.com/stablecoins" rel="noopener">DefiLlama</a>'s public data, against about $301.1bn a year earlier, a rise of roughly 4 per cent by our calculation. Rain's bank would hold reserves for other issuers as well as issuing its own coins. Gould has argued for keeping the charter route open. "New entrants into the federal banking sector are good for consumers, the banking industry and the economy," he said when the OCC conditionally approved a batch of charters in December.`,
    `Rain's application now goes through OCC review, including a public comment period. The ICBA has asked the court for an order that would stop the agency granting it, or anyone else, a charter under the rule being challenged. Rain has set out what its bank would and would not do in detail. The court will decide which side of the line those plans fall on.`,
  ] }),

MK({ slug:"sixty-nine-per-cent-from-one-customer", kick:"Data Centers",
  headline:"Sixty-nine per cent from one customer",
  dek:"DayOne, the Singapore data-centre builder that began inside China's GDS, has filed for a Nasdaq listing. Its prospectus says one short-video company supplies most of its revenue, two of its customers are on the Pentagon's Chinese military list, and members of Congress have already called it China-linked.",
  metaDesc:"DayOne Data Centers filed an F-1 for a Nasdaq IPO under DODC on 5 October 2026. H1 revenue was $512m; its largest customer, a short-form video company, supplied 69.2% of it. Two customers are on the US CMC List.",
  author:"marcus-oyelaran", date:"2026-10-06T16:35:00Z",
  capt:"Traffic at the crossing between Johor Bahru and Singapore. DayOne built its business on the two sides of that border, and Malaysia supplied 87 per cent of its revenue in the first half of 2026.",
  tags:[{name:"Capital Markets",slug:"capital-markets"},{name:"Data Centers",slug:"data-centers"},{name:"AI Infrastructure",slug:"ai-infrastructure"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/ai/nscales-backlog-has-two-names-on-it/",kick:"AI Infrastructure",title:"Nscale's backlog has two names on it",ago:"SEPTEMBER 22, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/the-dependency-runs-the-other-way/",kick:"AI Infrastructure",title:"The dependency runs the other way",ago:"SEPTEMBER 27, 2026"},
    {href:"/markets/ninety-cents-on-the-dollar/",kick:"AI Infrastructure",title:"Ninety cents on the dollar",ago:"SEPTEMBER 24, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `DayOne Data Centers, a Singapore company that builds data-centre campuses for the largest cloud and technology groups, filed on Monday to list American depositary shares on Nasdaq under the symbol DODC. Its <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2118192/000119312526414188/d223752df1.htm" rel="noopener">registration statement on Form F-1</a> leaves the number of shares and the price range blank. Morgan Stanley, J.P. Morgan, BofA Securities, Citigroup and BNP Paribas are the underwriters. The company filed confidentially in August for an offering that may raise as much as $5bn at a valuation of about $20bn, Bloomberg has reported, in a story carried by <a href="https://www.straitstimes.com/business/singapores-dayone-files-for-us-ipo-joining-data-centre-rush" rel="noopener">The Straits Times</a>.`,
    `The growth is real. Revenue for the six months to June was $512.0m, against $151.5m a year earlier; for all of 2025 it was $484.3m, up from $178.1m in 2024. The net loss for the half was $77.2m. The loss for 2025, $367.1m, was mostly share-based compensation, which came to $341.8m that year. DayOne says it had 962MW of capacity in service on 20 September and about 2.3GW of what it calls Bookings, substantially all of which it expects to deliver by the end of 2028.`,
    `"DayOne is a leading digital infrastructure platform outside of the United States and China," the prospectus summary begins. The risk factors describe who pays for it. In 2025 and in the first half of 2026 its largest customer, which the filing describes as a global technology company with a short-form video platform, accounted for 69.4 per cent and 69.2 per cent of revenue. The second-largest supplied 12.3 per cent and 15.1 per cent. On our arithmetic, two customers produced 84.3 per cent of first-half revenue. The filing does not name either of them. Malaysia, home to DayOne's campuses in Johor across the strait from Singapore, produced 87.0 per cent of revenue in the half.`,
    `The filing links that concentration to geopolitics itself. "Moreover, the ongoing geopolitical tensions could potentially impact the business of our largest customer, a global technology company with a leading short-form video platform serving a worldwide audience," it says. DayOne adds that it does not believe the tensions would affect its existing contracts with that customer, but that they could affect future deployments. "If our largest customer were to become subject to any such restrictions, its growth prospects may be adversely affected, thereby resulting in reduced demand for our data center services."`,
    `Two more disclosures sit a few pages later. "For example, two of our existing customers have been added to the list of Chinese Military Companies maintained by the U.S. Department of Defense," the prospectus says. "In addition, our supply chain may from time to time include modular components that are manufactured by entities on the Entity List." It does not say which customers, or what share of revenue they account for, a gap the <a href="https://www.businesstimes.com.sg/companies-markets/singapore-data-centre-operator-dayone-opts-us-ipo-likely-us20-billion-valuation" rel="noopener">Business Times</a> also noted. It also discloses that on 30 October 2025 several members of Congress wrote to the Commerce Department recommending that it investigate and restrict adversary products in AI infrastructure among other industries, in a letter that, by DayOne's account, described it as a China-linked company operating in the AI sector. DayOne says it is not aware of any action resulting from the letter.`,
    `DayOne was incorporated in the Cayman Islands in May 2022 as DigitalLand Holdings, a consolidated subsidiary of GDS Holdings, the Chinese data-centre operator listed in New York and Hong Kong. It was deconsolidated at the end of 2024 and took its current name on 1 January 2025. GDS still owns 19.4 per cent, behind Coatue and Hillhouse, which hold 19.5 per cent each, and ahead of Achi at 15.6 per cent, according to the shareholder table. GDS's chairman and chief executive, William Wei Huang, sits on DayOne's board. The filing notes that DayOne has no non-compete arrangement with GDS that would survive the offering, and that GDS could expand internationally and compete with it.`,
    `The prospectus addresses the <a href="/policy/two-hundred-thousand-for-a-92478-stake/">outbound investment</a> rule, the Treasury regulation that bars or requires notice of some American investments in Chinese companies working on chips, quantum and AI. "We do not believe we are a Covered Foreign Person under the Final Rule, because we do not engage in any Covered Activity (as defined in the Final Rule) or otherwise meet the definition of Covered Foreign Persons provided in the Final Rule," it says. It then notes that the rule is under review and that the COINS Act, now law, requires new regulations to replace it. "Changes to the publicly traded securities exception or other aspects of the Final Rule or the COINS Act could prohibit the purchase or trading of our ADSs by U.S. persons, impose new notification or other regulatory requirements, or make our ADSs less attractive to certain investors."`,
    `The money question is less dramatic but just as large. DayOne estimates the cost of completing its current Bookings at about $11.4bn beyond what it had spent by June. It paid $3.11bn for property, equipment, land use rights and construction deposits in the first half alone, about six times its revenue for the period on our arithmetic. At the end of June it had $1.98bn in cash, $1.4bn of short-term investments, $1.82bn of undrawn credit facilities and $4.35bn of long-term borrowings. "We believe that our currently available capital resources will be sufficient to fully fund our current Bookings," the filing says, adding that new Bookings will need more equity or debt.`,
    `DayOne is one of a group of data-centre companies preparing to list. Switch is seeking a valuation approaching $50bn including debt, and Nscale could raise as much as $3bn, according to the Bloomberg report. DayOne's own model is a few very large tenants, most of them on agreements of 10 to 15 years, and a build programme that only keeps growing if those tenants keep signing. Its largest tenant is a company it does not name, and its prospectus says that tenant could be hit by American trade restrictions. American investors are being asked to buy the shares on those terms.`,
  ] }),

BU({ slug:"half-a-million-dollars-in-a-quarter", kick:"Semiconductors",
  headline:"Half a million dollars in a quarter",
  dek:"Blaize, the AI chip company that listed through a SPAC last year, says it booked about $0.5m of revenue in the third quarter and cut its outlook again. The full-year number now depends on a customer that has paid $0.2m of a $13.3m bill due in September, and on inventory the company needs cash to buy.",
  metaDesc:"Blaize Holdings said on 5 October 2026 it expects about $0.5m of Q3 revenue and cut 2026 guidance to $32m-$36m from $40m-$43m. Customer NeoTensr paid $0.2m of a $13.3m receivable; DOJ and SEC inquiries are disclosed.",
  author:"jonathan-bright", date:"2026-10-06T17:50:00Z",
  capt:"An integrated circuit on a printed circuit board. Blaize designs its own Graph Streaming Processor for AI inference, but most of the revenue in its outlook comes from servers it must first buy the parts for.",
  tags:[{name:"Semiconductors",slug:"semiconductors"},{name:"Capital Markets",slug:"capital-markets"},{name:"Risk",slug:"risk"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/ai/the-guidance-that-assumes-china-buys-nothing/",kick:"Semiconductors",title:"The guidance that assumes China buys nothing",ago:"SEPTEMBER 24, 2026"},
    {href:"/ai/gpu-cloud-market-shakeout/",kick:"Analysis",title:"The GPU cloud market enters its shakeout phase",ago:"JULY 11, 2026"},
    {href:"/markets/ninety-cents-on-the-dollar/",kick:"AI Infrastructure",title:"Ninety cents on the dollar",ago:"SEPTEMBER 24, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `Blaize Holdings, a California designer of AI inference chips that went public through a merger with the blank-cheque company BurTech Acquisition in January 2025, told investors late on Monday that it expects to report revenue of about $0.5m for the three months to September. A <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1871638/000187163826000063/blz-20261005.htm" rel="noopener">Form 8-K</a> filed that evening says all of it was earned in August, from hardware sold to one customer in Europe. Blaize cut its full-year revenue guidance to between $32.0m and $36.0m. In mid-August it had said $40.0m to $43.0m.`,
    `Blaize shares closed on Monday at 43.6 cents. By early afternoon in New York on Tuesday they were changing hands at about 29 cents, a third lower, on <a href="https://www.nasdaq.com/market-activity/stocks/bzai" rel="noopener">Nasdaq</a> data. In May the company sold 18.9 million new shares to the public at $1.85 each; on our arithmetic the stock is now about 84 per cent below that price. Rosenblatt's Kevin Cassidy downgraded the stock to neutral with a 40-cent target, and Craig-Hallum's Richard Shannon moved to hold, according to an Investing.com report carried by <a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/blaize-shares-plunge-guidance-cut-143327488.html" rel="noopener">Yahoo Finance</a>, with Cassidy pointing to limited cash, tight inventory and longer supplier lead times.`,
    `Blaize booked $14.7m of revenue in the first half, according to its <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1871638/000187163826000044/ex991-2026xq2pressrelease.htm" rel="noopener">second-quarter release</a>. Add the third quarter's $0.5m and, on our calculation, the guidance requires $16.8m to $20.8m of revenue in the final three months of the year. That is more than it recognised in the first nine months combined.`,
    `The 8-K says where that revenue is supposed to come from. "The revised guidance is based on binding, non-cancellable purchase orders from NeoTensr and a new purchase order from an existing customer in process of signature." The NeoTensr orders sit under a contract signed in April for up to $50.0m, which was amended on 7 August into a take-or-pay arrangement with deliveries of servers intended to be completed by July 2027, according to the company's <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1871638/000187163826000046/blz-20260630.htm" rel="noopener">quarterly report</a>. NeoTensr has issued one purchase order under it, for $13.7m, and paid a 3 per cent deposit.`,
    `The balance of that order, $13.3m, was due on 24 September. "On September 30, 2026, NeoTensr paid $0.2 million toward this balance, and the Company has agreed to extend the terms of this receivable, which is now expected to be paid during the fourth quarter of 2026," the filing says. "The Company has sufficient inventory on hand to partially fulfill the orders in the fourth quarter and is working with suppliers to obtain the balance before the year end." And: "Should there be delays in timing or in receiving payments from customers scheduled during the fourth quarter, the Company's revenue recognition for the year could be adversely affected."`,
    `That is a circular problem. "The revision reflects the Company's updated expectations regarding shipment timing and management of working capital to meet inventory purchase needs," the filing says. To recognise the revenue Blaize has to ship servers, and to ship them it has to buy parts. To buy parts it needs cash, and the $13.1m NeoTensr still owes, on our arithmetic, is exactly the kind of cash that would pay for them. At the end of June Blaize had $36.8m in cash, about $12.4m of it in Chinese banks and subject to currency controls, and it had used $41.3m in operations in the first half. "Given the Company's liquidity condition, as described above, the Company will be unable to continue to operate as a going concern and continue its operations unless it raises additional financing, if such financing is available at all," the quarterly report says. It has a committed equity facility with about $15.5m of capacity left, but cannot draw on it while the shares trade below $1.`,
    `This has happened before. In 2025 Blaize signed a strategic agreement with Starshine Computing Power Technology, a Hong Kong company, under which Starshine agreed to deliver at least $120.0m of revenue over 18 months, subject to purchase orders. Starshine issued one order, for $10.4m, and paid $1.6m. The remaining $8.8m has been handed to a collections agent that would keep 10 per cent of anything it recovers, and Blaize says it does not expect further orders. NeoTensr's first order, $23.8m in the fourth quarter of 2025, was paid in full this year. The quarterly report's receivables table lists a balance of the same size at the end of 2025 from a customer located in China; we infer it is NeoTensr's, though the table does not use names.`,
    `The company's filings add the other pressures. In July and August the Justice Department sent subpoenas and inquiries to Blaize and some of its employees and directors, seeking information about its business and its agreements with certain counterparties, and the SEC has made voluntary requests for similar material. Blaize says it is cooperating and cannot predict the outcome. Two proposed shareholder class actions, filed on 4 August and 17 September, allege that the company and some of its officers made false and misleading statements or omissions about the Starshine and NeoTensr contracts; the second also names the underwriters of the May offering. Blaize says the cases are at a preliminary stage. On 24 September Blaize began cutting about 26 per cent of its staff, a move it expects to save $7.5m to $8.4m a year, and on 28 September Nasdaq told it the closing bid price had been below $1 for 30 consecutive business days, giving it until 29 March 2027 to recover.`,
    `In August, chief financial officer Harminder Sehmi said: "Our revised full-year revenue outlook is weighted toward revenue from our largest customer and is based on binding, non-cancellable purchase orders that we believe we can fulfill with inventory commitments already made or planned." Chief executive Dinakar Munagala said then: "What changed is the pace at which opportunity converts into orders, alongside materially higher memory pricing." Monday's filing uses almost the same basis for a lower number, and the largest customer has since missed its payment date. At about 29 cents, and the 144.8 million shares the company had outstanding in mid-August, the market is valuing Blaize at about $42m on our arithmetic. That is not much more than the revenue the company still says it will book this year.`,
  ] }),

CY({ slug:"patched-by-wednesday-rebooting-by-friday", kick:"Vulnerabilities",
  headline:"Patched by Wednesday, rebooting by Friday",
  dek:"Federal agencies had until 30 September to put Citrix's fix on their NetScaler gateways. By Friday the fixed build was crashing under a new attack. Citrix has now patched its third exploited NetScaler zero-day in a week, and CISA wants it in by Wednesday, with a forensic check, for a flaw Citrix describes as a denial-of-service bug.",
  metaDesc:"Citrix patched CVE-2026-88779, a NetScaler SAML denial-of-service zero-day, over the weekend of 3-4 October 2026, days after fixing CVE-2026-88771 and 88772. CISA added it to KEV the same day with a 7 October deadline and a forensic triage requirement.",
  author:"sam-porter", date:"2026-10-06T16:35:00Z",
  capt:"Network equipment glowing in a dark rack. A NetScaler gateway is the front door for remote users: when it crashes, the services behind it go dark with it.",
  tags:[{name:"Cybersecurity",slug:"cybersecurity"},{name:"Networking",slug:"networking"},{name:"Threat Intelligence",slug:"threat-intelligence"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/cybersecurity/three-days-to-patch-the-gateway/",kick:"Vulnerabilities",title:"Three days to patch the gateway",ago:"SEPTEMBER 28, 2026"},
    {href:"/cybersecurity/the-ninth-time-this-year/",kick:"Vulnerabilities",title:"The ninth time this year",ago:"OCTOBER 1, 2026"},
    {href:"/cybersecurity/fixed-in-july-explained-on-wednesday-attacked-on-thursday/",kick:"Vulnerabilities",title:"Fixed in July, explained on Wednesday, attacked on Thursday",ago:"OCTOBER 3, 2026"},
  ],
  body:[
    `Wednesday 30 September was the deadline. CISA had given federal civilian agencies three days to fix two actively exploited flaws in Citrix NetScaler ADC and NetScaler Gateway, and <a href="/cybersecurity/three-days-to-patch-the-gateway/">the fixed builds</a> were 14.1-73.37 and 13.1-64.23. On Friday 2 October, administrators who had installed them started reporting that their appliances were rebooting on their own. Some had been rebuilt from fresh images. They rebooted too.`,
    `By Sunday morning Citrix had a name for it, CVE-2026-88779, and another set of builds: 14.1-73.41 and 13.1-64.28, with separate FIPS editions. The <a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-88779" rel="noopener">NVD record</a> was published at 04:16 UTC on 4 October. CISA put the flaw into its <a href="https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog" rel="noopener">Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue</a> the same day, with a due date of Wednesday 7 October. It is the third NetScaler zero-day Citrix has confirmed in a week, and the sixth NetScaler entry the catalogue has taken in 2026.`,
    `The instruction that matters most to anyone who did the work last week came from Citrix, in its <a href="https://community.citrix.com/techzone-blogs/110_security-updates/understanding-and-addressing-cve-2026-88779-in-citrix-netscaler-adc-and-citrix-netscaler-gateway/" rel="noopener">blog post</a> and as quoted by <a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/citrix-patches-netscaler-saml-zero-day-exploited-in-attacks/" rel="noopener">BleepingComputer</a>: "If you upgraded your NetScaler deployment with one of the updated software releases identified in the security bulletin for CVE 2026-88771 through CVE 2026-88778, and if you have determined that your NetScaler deployment meets the preconditions describe above, please upgrade your deployment again with the software released as part of the security bulletin for CVE-2026-88779." The precondition is SAML, the single sign-on standard. An appliance is exposed if it is configured as a SAML service provider or a SAML identity provider, which an administrator can check for in two lines of configuration.`,
    `Citrix describes the flaw as an availability problem. "Citrix has observed targeted attacks on unmitigated NetScaler deployments which can lead to Denial of Service," the company said in a blog post. "If the condition is triggered repeatedly, the service may remain unavailable. Our analysis indicates that this issue affects service availability, and we have not identified an impact on the integrity of customer data." The scores agree. Citrix rates it 8.7 out of 10 under version 4 of the scoring system; NVD, under version 3.1, gives it 7.5, with high impact on availability and none on confidentiality or integrity.`,
    `The traffic says the attackers were hoping for more. One administrator, BleepingComputer reported, who went through the logs found crafted login usernames containing shell commands to download a file from a remote address, save it and run it, immediately before three crash sequences. According to <a href="https://www.securityweek.com/exploitation-of-citrix-netscaler-zero-day-hits-appliances-patched-days-earlier/" rel="noopener">SecurityWeek</a>, a user who obtained the script said it tries to plant web shells, survive reboots and upload the appliance's configuration and backups, while cautioning that there was no proof it ran. Kevin Beaumont, the researcher who had named last week's pair "PitScaler", watched patched honeypots crash and then found one running something. "So on one of the honeypots it's running a downloaded (malware) binary. Both were patched, so new vuln," he wrote.`,
    `watchTowr, the security firm that reproduced the flaw, has reached a narrower conclusion. It told SecurityWeek that CVE-2026-88779 is a denial-of-service bug that can only crash systems, and that it suspects attackers have used it to crash machines on purpose to make exploitation of CVE-2026-88771, last week's input-validation flaw, faster. The new bug also only affects appliances with SAML configured. "This means it doesn't work out of the box against every NetScaler deployment," Jake Knott, its head of threat intelligence, told <a href="https://cyberscoop.com/citrix-netscaler-third-exploited-zero-day-vulnerability/" rel="noopener">CyberScoop</a>. "While this is very inconvenient, it doesn't have organizations scrambling to trigger incident response." Joe Toomey of the cyber insurer Coalition read the same payloads differently: they "clearly contain shellcode that implies that the threat actor believes they can use this vulnerability, or chain it with another vulnerability, in order to achieve remote-code execution."`,
    `CISA has not split the difference. The catalogue entry calls it a flaw "that could allow for a denial of service", and then marks it, like last week's two, for forensic triage. Under Binding Operational Directive 26-04 that means an agency has three days to fix the appliance and must also carry out a forensic triage to check whether it has been compromised, for a bug whose vendor says it has found no impact on the integrity of customer data. On the evidence above, that is not excessive. A crash is an event a defender sees; a quiet compromise is not. If watchTowr is right and the crashes are being used to speed up the earlier exploit, then an appliance that rebooted last week is exactly the one that needs looking at.`,
    `It also explains why a two-day gap between deadline and attack matters more than the bug's score. The earlier pair were serious: Mandiant found that campaign "ongoing since at least early September", and Palo Alto Networks counted roughly 50,000 potentially exposed NetScaler instances as of 27 September, according to <a href="https://www.securityweek.com/government-finance-orgs-targeted-in-weeks-long-netscaler-zero-day-attacks/" rel="noopener">SecurityWeek</a>. Agencies and companies that rushed to install 14.1-73.37 did the right thing, and the attackers moved straight onto the build they had installed.`,
    `Citrix handled this round faster, and researchers have said so. It warned customers on Friday and shipped a fix within about two days. "After we were alerted to this issue we immediately developed and published a mitigation while concurrently developing, testing and deploying a fix," a spokesperson said in a statement, CyberScoop reported. The company declined to say how many customers were hit or when exploitation began. Toomey's verdict was mixed: "Although it's difficult to celebrate given this is the third publicly-exploited NetScaler zero-day vulnerability in a two-week window, it is a step in the right direction."`,
    `For a security team, the arithmetic is the hard part. Two emergency upgrades of the same edge appliance in a week for three exploited flaws, two forensic checks, two three-day patch deadlines, and a front door that, when attacked, locks out the staff who need to get in. The deadline on Wednesday binds only federal civilian agencies. Everyone else running SAML on a NetScaler is on the same clock, without anyone setting it.`,
  ] }),

BU({ slug:"nineteen-months-three-million-texans", kick:"Data Breach",
  headline:"Nineteen months, three million Texans",
  dek:"Oracle learned of the theft of patient data from old Cerner servers in February 2025 and told hospitals that notifying patients was their job. On Friday a filing under Cerner's name appeared on the Texas attorney general's breach list: 2,992,244 Texans, Social Security numbers included, letters sent by mail.",
  metaDesc:"A Texas attorney general listing published 2 October 2026 shows Cerner Corporation reported a breach affecting 2,992,244 Texans. Bloomberg reports the 2025 Oracle Health breach hit nearly 20 million people. Oracle declined to comment.",
  author:"jonathan-bright", date:"2026-10-06T16:45:00Z",
  capt:"A close-up of a rack of computer equipment. The data was taken from an old Cerner server that Oracle had not yet moved to its cloud, according to its 2025 notice to hospital customers.",
  tags:[{name:"Cybersecurity",slug:"cybersecurity"},{name:"Compliance",slug:"compliance"},{name:"Enterprise Software",slug:"enterprise-software"}],
  related:[
    {href:"/cybersecurity/zero-day-or-on-the-list-since-june/",kick:"Data Breach",title:"A zero-day, or a flaw on the list since June",ago:"SEPTEMBER 28, 2026"},
    {href:"/cybersecurity/everyone-shares-the-same-vendors/",kick:"Risk",title:"Everyone shares the same vendors",ago:"SEPTEMBER 7, 2026"},
    {href:"/cybersecurity/critical-infrastructure-reporting-rules/",kick:"Regulation",title:"Incident reporting clocks start ticking for critical infrastructure",ago:"NOVEMBER 19, 2025"},
  ],
  body:[
    `The Texas attorney general keeps a public list of data breaches that affect at least 250 Texans. Search it for "Oracle" and nothing comes back. Search it for "Cerner" and there is one entry: Cerner Corporation, 2800 Rockcreek Parkway, Kansas City, Missouri. Information affected: address, Social Security number, medical information. Texans affected: 2,992,244. Notice provided to consumers: yes, by U.S. mail. Date published on the <a href="https://oag.my.site.com/datasecuritybreachreport/apex/DataSecurityReportsPage" rel="noopener">attorney general's site</a>: 2 October 2026, a Friday.`,
    `Cerner is the electronic health records company Oracle bought in 2022 for $28bn and now runs as Oracle Health. The entry puts a number, for one state, on a breach whose size Oracle did not give in its 2025 notice to customers. On Monday <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-05/oracle-2025-health-breach-compromised-data-of-20-million-people" rel="noopener">Bloomberg</a> reported, citing information released by the Texas attorney general, that the incident compromised data belonging to nearly 20 million people. Oracle declined to comment further on Monday, according to <a href="https://moneycheck.com/oracle-orcl-health-unit-breach-compromised-20-million-records-texas-ag-reveals/" rel="noopener">MoneyCheck</a>, which carried Bloomberg's report; <a href="https://gizmodo.com/oracle-health-data-breach-from-last-year-was-reportedly-huge-2000822029" rel="noopener">Gizmodo</a>, which also summarised it, said it had asked Oracle for a statement. The public listing this desk read shows only the Texas figure, which is what the state's form asks for. If the national number is right, Texans are about 15 per cent of the total, on our arithmetic.`,
    `The breach itself is old news to the hospitals. In March 2025 <a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/oracle-health-breach-compromises-patient-data-at-us-hospitals/" rel="noopener">BleepingComputer</a> reported a notice Oracle Health had sent to affected customers. "We are writing to inform you that, on or around February 20, 2025, we became aware of a cybersecurity event involving unauthorized access to some amount of your Cerner data that was on an old legacy server not yet migrated to the Oracle Cloud," it read. Oracle said an attacker had used compromised customer credentials to get in at some point after 22 January 2025 and copied data to a remote server. Hospitals were being extorted for millions of dollars in cryptocurrency by someone calling himself "Andrew", BleepingComputer's sources said.`,
    `What made the 2025 notice unusual was who it left holding the job. Oracle Health told hospitals it would not notify patients directly, according to BleepingComputer: it was for each hospital to decide whether the theft was a breach under HIPAA, the federal health privacy law, and whether to send notices. Oracle offered to help identify affected people, supply templates, pay for credit monitoring and pay the mailing vendor. It would not send the letters on the hospitals' behalf. Its communications came on plain paper rather than letterhead, the report said, signed by Seema Verma, Oracle Health's executive vice president and general manager, and customers were directed to talk to its security office by phone rather than email.`,
    `Friday's entry is a filing under Cerner's own name. Texas law makes that a specific obligation. Under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/BC/htm/BC.521.htm" rel="noopener">section 521.053</a> of the Business and Commerce Code, a business that has to notify people of a breach must tell the attorney general "as soon as practicable and not later than the 30th day after the date on which the person determines that the breach occurred" if at least 250 Texans are involved. The report must give the number of Texans affected, the number sent a notice by mail or another direct method, what the company has done and plans to do, and whether law enforcement is investigating. The attorney general publishes a list; the full report is not on it.`,
    `The list does not say when Cerner decided the breach had occurred, so it cannot show whether the filing was late. It shows dates. Oracle says it learned of the incident on or around 20 February 2025. The listing went up 589 days later, by our count. The site warns that details, "including number of affected Texans and whether notice was provided to them may change after a report is listed here". It records that notice was given by mail. It does not say who sent the letters: Cerner, the hospitals, or the mailing vendor Oracle offered to pay for.`,
    `One confusion is worth clearing up, because some coverage this week has run the two together. In March 2025 a hacker using the name rose87168 claimed to have stolen six million records from Oracle Cloud's single sign-on servers. Oracle told <a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/oracle-denies-data-breach-after-hacker-claims-theft-of-6-million-data-records/" rel="noopener">BleepingComputer</a>: "There has been no breach of Oracle Cloud. The published credentials are not for the Oracle Cloud. No Oracle Cloud customers experienced a breach or lost any data." That was a separate claim about a separate system. The Oracle Health notice was careful in a different way: the server was "not yet migrated to the Oracle Cloud". Both statements can be true at once, and the second is how a breach at one of the largest cloud companies happened outside its cloud.`,
    `For Oracle's customers the number changes the conversation. Bloomberg notes that Oracle Health's clients include the Department of Defense and the Department of Veterans Affairs. Hospitals that were told in 2025 that patient notification was their job have spent a year and a half dealing with the consequences of a breach of a vendor's server. A count has now appeared, and it did not come from Oracle's own announcements. It is a row in a Texas database, under the name of the company Oracle bought.`,
  ] }),

];
